\subtree{
\title{E1-F1 .. E1-F6: a bound variable is typed by its binder's DECLARED type (agent E1, 2026-11-24)}

\p{Findings of package E1 (board 20:10Z - 20:35Z, \src{docs/design/review/eager-reduction.md} 6.6, \code{\verb~|work/e1/patches/E1-F2-F5-design.md~}): the machine accepted exports the official checker rejects because a type that is only CONVERTIBLE with the official one was used in its place (the official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not transitive: package S's triple \code{\verb~|d intro = d h~}, \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~} accepted, \code{\verb~|d h = 5~} rejected). 78 exports \code{\verb~|e1-*~} of the S manifest (official verdicts from Docker; generator \code{\verb~|src/test/resources/s/gen/batchE.py~}), all strict rows.}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|#~}\th{Where}\th{Was}\th{Now}}
\tr{\td{F1}\td{\code{\verb~|Infer.letBind~}, \code{\verb~|Values.mkLet~}}\td{the let variable (a LET suspension, never a variable) had the type of its VALUE}\td{the suspension carries \code{\verb~|EVAL(A)~}, the value of the DECLARED type, in \code{\verb~|M_TYPE~} (\code{\verb~|vtypeFast~} answers a SUSP from it); one record per declared type (\code{\verb~|aux = SUSP_LET + j~}); the declared type is evaluated in ONLY mode too (\code{\verb~|letOnly~}, frame INFER2\verb~|[~13\verb~|]~ with \code{\verb~|Tv = 0~}); INFER2\verb~|[~14\verb~|]~ carries \code{\verb~|EVAL(A)~}}}
\tr{\td{F2, F3}\td{\code{\verb~|Eval.instBody~} (\code{\verb~|INST(PII, x)~}: the type of a lambda instantiated = its body typed again under \code{\verb~|x~})}\td{the lambda's variable had the type of whatever \code{\verb~|x~} is (the argument of a redex; the comparison variable of CBIND, typed by the other side's domain)}\td{if the body's type can depend on the type of its variable (\code{\verb~|Infer.typeReads~}, syntactic) and \code{\verb~|x~} does not have exactly \code{\verb~|DOM(PII)~} as its type (\code{\verb~|vtypeFast(x) == DOM~}), the variable is bound to a TYPED BINDING (\code{\verb~|Values.mkTyped~}: a plain SUSP, aux \code{\verb~|SUSP_TYPED~}, \code{\verb~|M_VAL = x~}, \code{\verb~|M_TYPE = DOM~}; EVAL peeks it to \code{\verb~|x~}, INFER reads the domain, frames keep it). \code{\verb~|DOM~} may need the judgment: frame INST\verb~|[~3\verb~|]~. \code{\verb~|Eval.force~} / \code{\verb~|forceFast~} return the value of a typed binding's value and do not memo-check it}}
\tr{\td{F4}\td{\code{\verb~|Infer.k_ck5~} (B9, \code{\verb~|expect~})}\td{the body was checked against the expected codomain under a variable of the lambda's OWN domain whenever the two domains were convertible}\td{B9 applies only when the lambda's domain IS the expected domain (one value); otherwise the generic rule (INFER + CONV, where CBIND compares under the expected domain and the lambda's type is instantiated under a typed binding). The frames CHECK2\verb~|[~2..5\verb~|]~ carry \code{\verb~|V~} instead of the tabling record (found again in \code{\verb~|k_ck6~}, \code{\verb~|ckRecord~})}}
\tr{\td{F5}\td{\code{\verb~|Infer.k_ck8~}}\td{the EXPECTED body type (when closed) was stored as the COD memo of the lambda's PII}\td{not stored (the memo written by the lam rule of INFER, the body's own closed type, stays)}}
\tr{\td{F6}\td{\code{\verb~|Reduce.k_iota3~}, \code{\verb~|Eval.apply~} / \code{\verb~|k_evalHead~}, \code{\verb~|Infer.Ext.zetaInexact~}, \code{\verb~|Infer.LET_EXACT~}}\td{the K rule typed the major premise by its VALUE; under \code{\verb~|gated~} the value of \code{\verb~|f a~} with \code{\verb~|f~} a let variable has the variable reduced away, the official \code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~} types the TERM through the variable's declared type}\td{a LET suspension whose declared type is the very type of its value is marked (\code{\verb~|M1 = LET_EXACT~}); applying any other let variable sets \code{\verb~|zetaInexact~} for the declaration, and a K comparison after that is a DECLINE (\code{\verb~|E_LIMIT~}, fatal). Cost: the declines of this rule on the corpora (the gate of the publication names them)}}
}

\subtree{
\title{E1-F7 (kind T, tolerated by the practical policy; 2026-11-24 21:35Z): the type of a term the official kernel does NOT reduce}

\p{Owner's note 13 (22:25Z): a kind T accept is tolerated in the default configuration, so this family is documented (\src{docs/design/review/official-divergence.md}) and not worked on further. Three rows of the S manifest carry the kind mark \code{\verb~|T~} (the machine accepts, the official checker rejects, the statement is \code{\verb~|True~}; official verdicts from Docker, the reference engine rejects): \code{\verb~|e1-sib-proj-applied-let-pi-bad~} and \code{\verb~|e1-sib-proj-redex-pi-bad~} (v0 and every set), \code{\verb~|e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad~} (\code{\verb~|gated~} and the default set; v0 is right only because its tabling record of the argument holds the INFER type). Nine more rows \code{\verb~|e1-sib-*~} are strict.}

\p{Root cause: the official kernel infers the type of a term it has not reduced in two places - the RAW major premise in \code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~}, and the struct of a STUCK projection (\code{\verb~|reduce_proj~} fails, \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} returns the projection as it was; \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_proof_irrel~}, structure eta, the unit-like rule and eta expansion then call \code{\verb~|infer_type~} on it). That type goes through the DECLARED domain of an uncontracted beta-redex and the declared type of a let variable. The machine's \code{\verb~|VTYPE~} works on the VALUE, where the redex is contracted (\code{\verb~|Values.mkSuspAt~} also returns a known VAL in place of the suspension, and \code{\verb~|Eval.proj~} evaluates the struct), and gives the argument's own type, which is only convertible. The F6 rule above (decline of the K rule after the application of an inexact let variable) covers one shape only and is not robust in a warm session (a memoised evaluation does not pass \code{\verb~|Eval.apply~} again).}

\p{What an exact fix needs (not built): (a) \code{\verb~|VTYPE~} of a plain suspension = \code{\verb~|INFER(term, frame, ONLY)~} (what v0's tabling stores), not \code{\verb~|VTYPE(FORCE)~}; (b) a suspension is never replaced by its known VAL where it stands for an argument (\code{\verb~|mkSuspAt~}, \code{\verb~|mkSusp~}, \code{\verb~|Eval.inertHead~}'s PEEK), so that a bound variable keeps the term it stands for; (c) \code{\verb~|Eval.proj~} keeps the suspension of an active struct term instead of its value; (d) the same in the compiled paths (\code{\verb~|Code~}, \code{\verb~|Rules~}, \code{\verb~|Ops~}: \code{\verb~|mkSusp~}, strict projections). Measured for a decline rule instead (Mathlib slices 425,620 / Std 100,096 engine checks): declarations that apply an inexact let variable and then type a projection or run the K rule: 322 / 12; declarations that bind an inexact let: 3,531 / 335; neither covers the let-free shapes. Cost of F2 / F3: \code{\verb~|INST(PII, x)~} builds one frame and one record more when the typed binding is needed; of F4: lambdas whose domain is not the expected domain's value take the generic rule.}
}

\subtree{
\title{E1-F4 and the switch \code{\verb~|convexpect~} (2026-11-24 22:41Z)}

\p{The fix of F4 (B9 only when the two domains are one value) is the whole tick cost of the fix: Cedar slice 2.40 \%, Std 0.30 \%, Init 0.32 \%, Mathlib slices 0.33 \%; the typed bindings of F2 / F3 cost nothing. In warm wall clock (8 workers, native) it does not show on Std and cedar, so it stays in the default. \code{\verb~|convexpect~} (\code{\verb~|Op.SW_CONV_EXPECT~}, off) gives the old B9 back; the three rows \code{\verb~|e1-lam-fvar-type-in-conv-*-bad~} are kind T rows and are accepted with it.}
}
}
